  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">DRA acute joint response Mozambique floods</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Mozambique is experiencing extensive flooding with 699,924 people affected (CENOE 27 Jan 2026) and numbers are rising. 59% of those affected live in Gaza Province (412,817 individuals), with many internally displaced people in government accommodation centers. A joint Rapid Needs Assessment (RNA) conducted by the Humanitarian Partner Platform found that the most underserved need in Gaza is FSL, with 620,000 people in need (OCHA). The floods are causing severe livelihood losses, accelerating food insecurity and economic vulnerability. The RNA conducted in Chibuto, Ch&#xf3;kw&#xe8;, Xai-Xai, Guij&#xe1;, and Massingir districts reports near-total (&gt;90%) crop losses of maize, rice, beans and peanuts and livestock losses of 2-4 animals per household (HH) leading to loss of income and food stocks. In addition, prices spiked dramatically, e.g. maize prices went up 40-50%, which is confirmed by WFP market monitoring. 14 out of 19 focus groups indicated a reduced meal frequency of 1-2 times a day due to a lack of (access to) food. The needs are intensified by pre-existing vulnerabilities with 3.2 million people in IPC Phase 3 in Mozambique and 81% of Gaza&apos;s population relying on subsistence agriculture (INE, 2017; FAO, 2023). One of the main RNA recommendations is to provide unconditional cash to address immediate food and NFI needs, utilizing mobile money platforms. In Jan&#x2013;Feb 2026, Save the Children (SC) conducted a preliminary Market Feasibility and Risk Assessment (MFRA) in flood-affected districts including Xai-Xai, Macia, Ch&#xf3;kw&#xe8; and Guij&#xe1;. The MFRA confirmed markets remain functional, accessible, and well-supplied. Even if inflation is high, conditions for cash and voucher assistance are favourable: improving access presence of high number of traders, stable market prices, and strong presence of Financial Service Providers, particularly mobile money services such as M-Pesa.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">SC and local partner, Hikone, will both respond in Xai-Xai, Chokwe and Guija districts. To meet immediate life-saving needs, SC will provide one-off MPCA at secured locations to 1100 displaced HHs through M-Pesa transfers. SC will conduct a market analysis, protection and gender risk analysis, community sensitization, and beneficiary verification applying vulnerability criteria before the transfers and PDMs will be conducted afterwards. The transfer value (5,000 MZN/HH) and targeting are agreed with INAS/INGD in line with cash working group guidelines. Cash is the preferred modality for affected population and government.  Hikone will further provide FSL assistance to displaced HHs in accommodation centres and communities, with 900 vulnerable HHs receiving emergency food kits and 800HHs who have lost their livelihoods (crop/livestock) receiving agriculture kits. Kits content is per guidelines from INGD/FSL Cluster and procured in Maputo to ensure quality.</narrative>
  </description>
